# Copy to .env (git-ignored). Dev-only defaults; use strong values in any shared environment.
DB_NAME=gestor_causas
DB_USERNAME=gestor
DB_PASSWORD=gestor
# Encrypts TOTP secrets at rest (ADR-0009): 32 random bytes in base64, e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# The dev profile has a fictitious default; any other environment must set its own.
# TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Shared password of the fictitious users created by the `demo` profile (ADR-0011), 12+ characters.
# Required when that profile is active; no default on purpose. Choose your own local value.
# DEMO_PASSWORD=
# Optional AI assistance (ADR-0033): a Google AI Studio key (https://aistudio.google.com/apikey).
# Leave it empty to turn the AI off for everyone. The free tier may use the texts to improve
# Google's products: fictitious data only until the legal review says otherwise.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-lite
# Behind a reverse proxy (production), set it to `native` so the login throttle and the public
# calculator's rate limit (ADR-0041) see each visitor's address instead of the proxy's. Only with a
# proxy that overwrites X-Forwarded-For: otherwise clients could spoof their address. The `prod`
# profile already sets it.
# SERVER_FORWARD_HEADERS_STRATEGY=native
# Outbound email (ADR-0048, docs/HOSTING.md "Correo saliente"). Leave MAIL_SMTP_HOST empty and
# nothing is sent: each email is logged without its body. Locally, start Mailpit with
# `docker compose --profile mail up -d mailpit` and read the emails at http://localhost:8025:
# MAIL_SMTP_HOST=localhost
# MAIL_SMTP_PORT=1025
# MAIL_SMTP_SECURITY=NONE                  # STARTTLS (default, port 587) or TLS (port 465) with a provider
# MAIL_SMTP_USERNAME=
# MAIL_SMTP_PASSWORD=                      # the provider's SMTP key: platform secrets only
# MAIL_FROM=avisos@ejemplo.test            # an address of a domain verified with the provider
#
# --- Production (T65, docs/HOSTING.md) --------------------------------------------------------------
# The Docker image (backend/Dockerfile) runs the `prod` profile: Secure cookies, Flyway only, no demo
# data, forwarded headers on. Set these in the hosting platform's secret settings, never in a file:
# SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod              # set by the image; never add dev or demo (startup fails)
# DB_URL=jdbc:postgresql://<host>:5432/<db>?sslmode=require   # JDBC form, not postgres://
# DB_USERNAME=
# DB_PASSWORD=
# TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=                     # its own key: the dev key is refused; keep a copy offline
# DB_POOL_SIZE=5                           # connections; free and small databases allow few
# PORT=8081                                # most platforms set it themselves
# GEMINI_API_KEY=                          # leave empty with real cases (see above)
#
# First firm and owner on an empty production database (T66, docs/HOSTING.md): a one-time run with
# SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod,bootstrap. It prints the owner's temporary password once and exits;
# it refuses once any firm exists. Pass them to that run only, not to the platform's settings.
# BOOTSTRAP_FIRM_NAME=Estudio Ejemplo
# BOOTSTRAP_FIRM_SLUG=estudio-ejemplo        # what users type as "estudio" to log in
# BOOTSTRAP_OWNER_EMAIL=titular@ejemplo.test
# BOOTSTRAP_OWNER_NAME=Titular Ejemplo
#
# Backups (scripts/backup.*, docs/OPERATIONS.md): folder (default ./backups, ideally on another,
# encrypted disk) and how many backups of each database to keep.
# BACKUP_DIR=D:/copias/gestor
# BACKUP_KEEP=14
